Each city is a node, and a `1` in the matrix means there is a direct connection. When an unvisited city is found, it starts one DFS and all reachable cities are marked. That whole DFS is one province.

The recursion is short and follows the connections until there are no new cities left.

```java
class Solution {
    public int numProvinces(int[][] adj) {
        int n = adj.length;
        boolean[] visited = new boolean[n];
        int provinces = 0;

        for (int city = 0; city < n; city++) {
            if (!visited[city]) {
                provinces++;
                dfs(city, adj, visited);
            }
        }

        return provinces;
    }

    private void dfs(int city, int[][] adj, boolean[] visited) {
        visited[city] = true;

        for (int next = 0; next < adj.length; next++) {
            if (adj[city][next] == 1 && !visited[next]) {
                dfs(next, adj, visited);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

For example, with connections between cities `0` and `1`, DFS from `0` visits both. If city `2` is separate, the outer loop starts another DFS, giving an answer of `2`.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because each visited row checks all possible neighbors.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the visited array and the recursion stack.

This version first finds indirect connections with the Floyd-Warshall idea. If city `i` can reach city `k` and city `k` can reach city `j`, then `i` can also reach `j`.

After that, one uncounted city marks all cities in its connected group as counted. This is slower than a normal traversal, but it is a different way to use the matrix directly.

```java
class Solution {
    public int numProvinces(int[][] adj) {
        int n = adj.length;
        boolean[][] connected = new boolean[n][n];

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                connected[i][j] = adj[i][j] == 1;
            }
        }

        for (int middle = 0; middle < n; middle++) {
            for (int from = 0; from < n; from++) {
                if (!connected[from][middle]) {
                    continue;
                }

                for (int to = 0; to < n; to++) {
                    if (connected[middle][to]) {
                        connected[from][to] = true;
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        boolean[] counted = new boolean[n];
        int provinces = 0;

        for (int city = 0; city < n; city++) {
            if (counted[city]) {
                continue;
            }

            provinces++;
            for (int next = 0; next < n; next++) {
                if (connected[city][next]) {
                    counted[next] = true;
                }
            }
        }

        return provinces;
    }
}
```

Dry run on three cities where `0` connects to `1` and `1` connects to `2`: closure adds the indirect connection from `0` to `2`. Starting with city `0` then marks all three cities, so only one province is counted.

The closure part takes `O(n^3)` time. The extra boolean matrices use `O(n^2)` space.

A queue works well here because it explores one whole group before moving to the next. Every time the outer loop sees an unvisited city, the province count increases and that city is added to the queue.

Cities are marked as soon as they enter the queue, so the same city is not added twice.

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Queue;

class Solution {
    public int numProvinces(int[][] adj) {
        int n = adj.length;
        boolean[] visited = new boolean[n];
        int provinces = 0;

        for (int start = 0; start < n; start++) {
            if (visited[start]) {
                continue;
            }

            provinces++;
            Queue<Integer> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
            queue.offer(start);
            visited[start] = true;

            while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
                int city = queue.poll();

                for (int next = 0; next < n; next++) {
                    if (adj[city][next] == 1 && !visited[next]) {
                        visited[next] = true;
                        queue.offer(next);
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return provinces;
    }
}
```

An empty matrix returns `0` naturally, since the loop does not run. A matrix with only diagonal `1`s gives one province per city.

The matrix still makes the running time `O(n^2)`. The visited array and queue use `O(n)` space.

I used disjoint set union here. At first, every city is its own group. For every direct connection in the upper half of the matrix, the two groups are merged. Each successful merge reduces the province count by one.

Only `j > i` is checked because the matrix represents an undirected connection, so checking both halves would repeat the same edge.

```python
class Solution:
    def numProvinces(self, adj):
        n = len(adj)
        parent = list(range(n))
        size = [1] * n
        provinces = n

        def find(city):
            if parent[city] != city:
                parent[city] = find(parent[city])
            return parent[city]

        def union(first, second):
            nonlocal provinces
            root_first = find(first)
            root_second = find(second)

            if root_first == root_second:
                return

            if size[root_first] < size[root_second]:
                root_first, root_second = root_second, root_first

            parent[root_second] = root_first
            size[root_first] += size[root_second]
            provinces -= 1

        for i in range(n):
            for j in range(i + 1, n):
                if adj[i][j] == 1:
                    union(i, j)

        return provinces
```

Dry run for `[[1,1,0],[1,1,0],[0,0,1]]`: start with `3` groups. The connection between `0` and `1` merges two groups, leaving `2`. City `2` has no connection to another city, so the answer stays `2`.

The matrix scan takes `O(n^2)` time. Union-find operations are almost constant on average, so the total is `O(n^2 * alpha(n))`. The parent and size arrays take `O(n)` space.

